  <text>
    <body>
      <div type="clause" n="81">
        <head><ref type="lawCode" target="law:IICn">II Cn</ref> 81</head>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="81" corresp="#wit-g-a">And drincelean ⁊ hlafordes rihtgifu stande æfre unawend.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="81" corresp="#wit-a">And drincelean ⁊ hlafordes rihtgyfu stande æfre unawend.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="81" corresp="#wit-q">Et drinceléan (id est retributio potus) et domini rectum donum semper stet inconuulsum.</ab>
        <ab type="editionText" xml:lang="ang" source="#robertson" n="80.1" corresp="#wit-b">⁊ dryncelean ⁊ hlafordes rihtgyfu stande æfre unawended.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#robertson" corresp="#wit-b">And there shall never be any interference with bargains successfully concluded or with the legal gifts made by a lord.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="de" source="#liebermann">Und ‘Trinklohn’ [Vertragsabschluss oder Nordisch Landgabe für Bewirthung] und des Herrn gesetzliche Schenkung bestehe immer unverrückt.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude" ana="#fromGerman">And let ‘drink-reward’ [conclusion of a contract, or in Norse usage a grant of land for hospitality] and the lord's lawful gift always stand unaltered.</ab>
        <note type="editorial">Liebermann compares III Atr 3.</note>
      </div>
    </body>
